{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790958562,"nonce":"eaKuYZUitWq36MgVnZOIlP6bjKQ=","title":"a String a plugin method returns reaches Haxe with its length where its pointer should be","message":"A plugin method whose Haxe extern returns `String` hands back the `*mut HaxeString` that `rayzor_plugin_string_new` made. The first use of that value in Haxe reads a pointer that is the string's length.\n\nReproducer: xwindow's generated Rayzor surface, packed with its adapter dylib (`rpkg pack --dylib ... --haxe-dir \u003cgenerated\u003e`).\n\n    var w = Window.open(new WindowAttributes());\n    var t = w.title(); // @:native(\"xwindow_window_title\") public function title():String;\n    trace(t.length);\n\n    rayzor: native crash signal=SIGSEGV fault=0x14 [outside registered JIT code]\n\nUnder lldb the fault is in `haxe_string_length`, `ldr x0, [x0, #0x8]` with `x0 = 0xc`: 12, the length of winit's default title \"winit window\". So the JIT passed the returned HaxeString's `len` (offset 8) as the string. The same program returns `Int`, `Float`, `Bool`, records, resources and C-like enums correctly, and reads variant fields through `Int` getters.\n\nThe method's descriptor has return tag 3 (PTR); the extern's own registration gives `String` the same tag. Where the call's result becomes a Haxe `String` is the place to look. xgpu's generated `String` getters (`GpuAdapter.name`, ...) will meet the same.","files":null}]}